 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Reset",
   "page": 6,
   "text": "Two resets clear all sixteen stages together, whatever the count. With AR (pin 5) low the power-on reset acts when supply is applied; with power up, a high on MR (pin 6) does the same. MR works independently of the automatic reset. While either reset is active the oscillator is stopped, so the part draws no switching power. AR high turns the power-on reset off and gives the lowest supply current."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Supply range and auto reset",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "With auto reset disabled (pin 5 at VDD) the part runs from 3.0 V to 18 V; with it enabled (pin 5 at VSS) from 8.5 V to 18 V."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Start of timing",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Once the reset is released the oscillator starts at the frequency the RC network sets, and the counter divides it, stage n running at fosc / 2^n. The counter advances on rising clock edges, and a clock conditioning stage accepts very slow clock edges."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Oscillator",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "The built-in RC oscillator needs one capacitor and two resistors. Below 10 kHz its accuracy is ±2% over temperature, ±20% over supply and ±3% over processing. An available clock can replace it: apply it to pin 3 and pins 1 and 2 become outputs."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Stage selection",
   "page": 6,
   "text": "A and B choose one of the stages 2^8, 2^10, 2^13 and 2^16 for the output. A high routes the 2^16 tap to the output for either state of B; with B low, though, the ninth stage takes its clock straight from the oscillator, so the output is in effect 2^8."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Output polarity",
   "page": 6,
   "text": "In reset, Q is low when Q/Q SEL is low and high when Q/Q SEL is high."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Recycle and single-cycle modes",
   "page": 6,
   "text": "MODE high passes the selected count to Q continuously, a 2^n divider. MODE low gives a single cycle: after reset the internal RS flip-flop clears and counting starts; after 2^(n−1) counts it sets and Q changes state, and it then stays put through the next 2^(n−1) counts and beyond. A master reset pulse or a change of the MODE level starts another cycle."
  }
 ],
